  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U5", "U3", "U7", "U8"],
      "pins": ["U5.19", "U5.20", "U5.21", "U5.6", "U5.22", "U3.5", "U7.5", "U8.5", "U3.6", "U7.6", "U8.6"],
      "nets": ["NET0004", "NET0012", "NET0010", "NET0009", "NET0005"],
      "observed": "On the Microcontroller sheet the shared SCK net (NET0004, tied to MAX31855 SCK pin 5 of U3/U7/U8) is routed to U5 pin 19 = GPIO4, while a chip-select net (NET0012, U8 CS pin 6) is routed to U5 pin 20 = GPIO5. The other two chip-selects are NET0009 -> U5 pin 6 = GPIO3 and NET0010 -> U5 pin 21 = GPIO6, and U5 pin 22 = GPIO7 is left unconnected. (MISO NET0005 is correctly on GPIO10.)",
      "expected": "The specification states the firmware uses SCK on GPIO5, MISO on GPIO10 and the three independent chip-selects on GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7 (\"SPI2 on GPIO5/GPIO10, CS pins GPIO4/6/7\"; \"sck on GPIO5 and miso on GPIO10; three independent CS Output pins on GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7\"). The drawing swaps SCK onto GPIO4, puts a CS onto GPIO5, uses GPIO3 for a CS instead of GPIO7, and leaves GPIO7 floating.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Reads up to 3 K-type thermocouples ... over a shared SPI bus (SCK/SO + 3 independent chip-selects CS0-CS2), confirmed by firmware/src/bin/main.rs (NUM_THERMOCOUPLES = 3, SPI2 on GPIO5/GPIO10, CS pins GPIO4/6/7)."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "SPI2 configured at 60 MHz, mode 0, with sck on GPIO5 and miso on GPIO10; three independent CS Output pins on GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch: U5 pin 19 GPIO04 -> NET0004 (the net on every MAX31855 SCK pin 5); U5 pin 20 GPIO05 -> NET0012 (U8 CS); U5 pin 6 GPIO03 -> NET0009 (U7 CS); U5 pin 21 GPIO06 -> NET0010 (U3 CS); U5 pin 22 GPIO07 unconnected"}
      ],
      "impact": "The firmware drives the SPI clock on GPIO5, which on this board is wired to a chip-select, and toggles GPIO4 as CS0, which is actually the SCK line; GPIO7's CS2 is never asserted and GPIO3 is wrongly used as a select. The thermocouple SPI bus cannot be operated as the firmware expects, so temperature reads fail or cause bus contention, defeating the three-zone sensing the board is built for.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The power, buck, power-mux, heater-switch and MISO wiring match the specification, but the thermocouple SPI bus is mis-routed on the MCU: SCK is on GPIO4 instead of GPIO5, a chip-select occupies GPIO5, another select uses GPIO3, and GPIO7 (intended CS2) is unconnected, contradicting the firmware's hard-coded GPIO map and breaking thermocouple sensing."
}

